{"schema_version": 4, "name": "recover-stochastic-abm", "investigation": "discovery-sbml-recovery", "status": "ran", "kind": "adversarial", "title": "Reject a stochastic system (no flat-SBML form)", "confidence": "Planned", "question": "Does the recovery pipeline correctly REFUSE to recover a flat SBML from a single\nlow-copy stochastic trajectory \u2014 recognizing the ABM/stochastic boundary where no\ndeterministic flat-SBML reaction network faithfully fits the data?\n", "hypothesis": "A single Gillespie trajectory at low copy numbers has noisy derivatives that no\nsmooth polynomial (mass-action) RHS can fit, so the non-recoverability detector\nflags it (worst-species R^2 well below threshold). The underlying network IS flat\nSBML only in the large-population mean-field limit; from this data it is not\nrecoverable. The adversarial passes by correctly REJECTING the system \u2014 the honest\nfailure that keeps the loop from overfitting noise.\n", "objective": "Run the non-recoverability detector on a single stochastic trajectory of the\nmass-action network and verify it is correctly flagged as not recoverable as a\ndeterministic flat SBML.\n", "description": "Adversarial / negative-control rung. The oracle is the SAME mass-action network as\nrecover-mass-action, but observed as one Gillespie trajectory at low copy numbers\n(recover-stochastic-abm). Deterministically it is a flat reaction network; as a\nsingle stochastic path it is not \u2014 its derivatives are dominated by shot noise. This\nis the ABM/stochastic boundary the sbml-decomposition investigation found (an ABM\nreduces to an SBML/ODE only in the large-population limit). The study is a control\nfor the whole ladder: the metric must pass by rejecting a system it should not claim\nto recover, so the recovery capabilities are not just curve-fitting anything.\n", "parent_studies": [{"study": "recover-oscillator", "relation": "builds-on"}], "baseline": [{"name": "baseline", "composite": "viva_sbml_decompositio